 Given the new auto-downsize feature is supposed to let images larger than `max_image_size_kb` through, the download cap should be raised above that setting (or made explicit), not silently frozen at 10MB."}, {"file": "app/controllers/uploads_controller.rb", "line": 66, "severity": "medium", "category": "bug", "title": "Auto-downsize loop ignores OptimizedImage.downsize return value and proceeds even on total failure", "body": "The while loop re-checks `tempfile.size > SiteSetting.max_image_size_kb.kilobytes` but never inspects the boolean returned by OptimizedImage.downsize. OptimizedImage.convert_with rescues every error and returns false on failure (e.g., ImageMagick not installed, corrupt source), but the controller discards that signal and only decrements `attempt`. If all five iterations fail, the upload proceeds with a file that may still exceed the configured limit — or be empty/corrupt from the failed in-place rewrite — and the user receives no indication that auto-resize failed."}, {"file": "app/controllers/uploads_controller.rb", "line": 64, "severity": "medium", "category": "bug", "title": "Auto-downsize gate uses filename extension instead of actual content type", "body": "The new block is gated on `FileHelper.is_image?(filename)`. For URL-sourced uploads the filename is derived from `File.basename(URI.parse(url).path)`, which frequently does not reflect the actual response (e.g., a JPEG served as `download.bin` or `attachment`). Real images with non-image filenames will skip auto-downsize and may exceed the configured limit, while non-image files masquerading as `.jpg` will be piped through OptimizedImage.downsize and likely corrupted by ImageMagick before being persisted."}]}
